  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Half-orcs are an easily-overlooked race. Their racial traits tend to\n\tpigeon-hole them into simple martial builds, and in most official settings and\n\tworks of fiction outside of D&D orcs have historically been portrayed barbaric\n\tsavages, so even characters’ backstories tend to be forced into an incredibly\n\tnarrow set of options. Half-orcs don’t get subraces, and unlike the half-elf\n\twe haven’t seen variants of any kind, so the race offers little flexibility.\n\tThere’s only so many times you can write a half-orc barbarian raised among\n\torcs or a half-orc fighter raised among humans and still have a memorable\n\tcharacter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "But just because a race’s built-in, published options are limited doesn’t\n\tmean that your story options are limited. It’s totally fine to play a half-orc\n\tbarbarian raised by their orcish family; there’s nothing wrong with the\n\tobvious character option, and being raised among orcs certainly doesn’t mean\n\tthat your character or their family have to conform to any preconcieved notion\n\tof what an orc is or does. But don’t be afraid to go for something unusual.\n\tMaybe your half-orc is an accountant and goes adventuring like real-world\n\tpeople go on weekend camping trips."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the half-orc is a simple choice. With no decision points and\n\twith the insurance provided by Relentless Endurance it’s a great option for\n\tnew players. But don’t let that appeal deter you if you’re a veteran: the\n\thalf-orc is still a solid racial option for some builds, and if you like big\n\tcritical hits, Savage Attacks is a nice damage bonus."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you absolutely love critical hits, a half-orc with some combination of\n\tBarbarian and Fighter (Champion) is a great build. Reckless Attack and\n\tImproved Critical make it very likely that you’ll score a critical hit in a\n\tturn, and with Savage Attacks, Brutal Critical, and a greataxe you’re rolling\n\ta bunch of d12’s all at once. Unfortunately, even the Custom Origin rules\n\tdon’t really broaden the half-orc’s class options. Your best bet is still to\n\twring a greataxe."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
