    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Half-Orc Classes (Customizable Origins)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the next section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The half-orc brings little to the Artificer except Relentless Endurance."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Combining Savage Attacks and Brutal Critical is immensely satisfying. The\n\tHalf-Orc’s extra skill also gives the Barbarian a much-needed button to push\n\toutside of combat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Relentless Endurance is great on a frail spellcaster, an extra skill is nice\n\ton a class which is frequently the party’s Face, and Darkvision is good if you\n\twant to sneak around in the dark."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Relentless Endurance is great because dropping to 0 as your party’s primary\n\thealer frequently means that the rest of your party is going to follow you to\n\tan early grave. Otherwise, there’s not much here."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Savage Attacks should work while using Wild Shape, but that’s really not\n\tenough to make the combination worthwhile. You’re probably your party’s\n\tprimary healer, so Relentless Endurance is good insurance for the whole\n\tparty."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A half-orc champion fighter is among the simplest and most straight-forward\n\tbuilds for new players. The expanded critical range makes Savage Attacks more\n\tuseful, and an extra skill gives you more options outside of combat. That\n\tsaid, despite this build’s simple effectiveness, it’s not very mechanically\n\tsatisfying so many experienced players quickly grow bored of it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Savage Attacks seems like it would be good on a monk, but many of your\n\tattacks will be made with unarmed strikes which will use small damage dice\n\tuntil you reach very high levels."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Without Reckless Attack or the Fighter’s huge number of attacks, Savage\n\tCritical is minimally useful. Your crits will be huge thanks to Divine Smite,\n\tbut they’ll still be infrequent."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Darkvision and an extra skill are useful on a class that’s typically\n\tstealthy, but that’s the only especially interesting combination here."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Darkvision and extra skill don’t hurt, but it’s easy to get that from other\n\traces. Savage Attacks could be interesting with an assassin, but the extra\n\tdamage on one or two attacks is minor for most of your career."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Relentless Endurance is nice, Darkvision saves you a spell, and an extra\n\tskill will help you expand beyond Face Skills. But you can get Darkvision and\n\ta skill from many races, and Relentless Endurance isn’t crucial."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Relentless Endurance is nice, Darkvision saves you an invocation unless you plan to rely on magical darkness, and an extra skill never hurts. "
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-warlock-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Hexblades using weapons can benefit from Savage Attacks when combined with Hexblade’s Curse. Warlocks have abundant options for getting Advantage on their attacks (Darkness, Shadow of Moil), so 19-20 critical hits with Advantage will make critical hits reasonably frequent. You can’t use Heavy weapons, so 1d10 is the biggest damage die you’ll get, and at once per short rest, that Hexblade’s Curse combo is good, but not enough to carry your build on its own. You’ll be good at eliminating high-priority enemies, but once Hexblade’s Curse is on cooldown you’re depending almost entirely on class features to get by."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Relentless Endurance is nice, Darkvision saves you a spell, and an extra\n\tskill will help you expand beyond Face Skills. But you can get Darkvision and\n\ta skill from many races, and Relentless Endurance isn’t crucial."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Half-Orc Classes (Default Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-default-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The most obvious option, the Half-Orc was tailor-made for the barbarian.\n\tStrength and Constitution are perfect Ability Score Increases, but the\n\tbenefits don’t stop there. Proficiency in Intimidation gives you an option in\n\tsocial situations where the Barbarian typically has little to do. Relentless\n\tEndurance will help keep you alive despite things like Reckless Attack, and\n\tSavage Attacks gives you a fun preview of Brutal Critical (and they stack,\n\twhich is even better)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Proficiency in Intimidation is nice, but it’s really not enough. You could\n\ttry a Valor Bard, but in medium armor a Strength-based build is very MAD\n\tbecause you need 14 in Dexterity and good scores in Strength, Constitution,\n\tand Charisma which is very hard to accomplish."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The normal Half-Orc doesn’t get a Wisdom increase, so the Cleric is a hard\n\tchoice. Mark of Finding considerably improves your viability as a cleric, but\n\tremember that Mark of Finding replaces all of your racial traits, so anything\n\tmechanical that you like about the Half-Orc goes away."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Savage Attacks and Relentless Endurance both work in wild shape, which makes\n\tthe standard Half-Orc slightly more lethal and more durable, so Circle of the\n\tMoon is an option. For other circles, consider Mark of Finding."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Half-Orc works for any fighter archetype. Strength-based builds are easy\n\tto do in heavy armor, and if you want to forgo a shield Relentless Endurance\n\twill help keep you alive on top of the Constitution increase. Savage Attacks\n\tis especially rewarding for Champions, and while you don’t get Reckless Attack\n\tor Brutal Critical like the Barbarian, the Fighter gets a total of 4 attacks\n\tat high levels, and if you Shove enemies prone you still get Advantage on your\n\tattacks against them so you can still make numerous high-damage critical\n\thits."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No increases to either Dexterity or Wisdom, and Strength-based monks simply\n\taren’t viable. Savage Attacks looks nice with the number of attacks you get\n\tfrom Flurry of Blows or Martial Arts, but monks get by on numerous small\n\tattacks rather than big, high-damage attacks."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Not such an easy choice as the Barbarian or the Fighter, but still a great\n\toption. While Charisma is very helpful for the Paladin, they don’t strictly\n\tneed an increase at 1st level. Strength and Constitution work fine, and\n\tIntimidation will save you a skill proficiency if you’re serving as your\n\tparty’s Face."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength-based builds are difficult, but possible. The Half-Orc would not be\n\tmy first choice for such a build, but it’s absolutely possible."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Dexterity increase, and Savage Attacks is much less interesting when\n\tyou’re not using a Greataxe."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Half-Orc Feats",
      "sourceAnchor": "feats",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-orcish-furyxgte",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Orcish FuryXGtE",
      "sourceAnchor": "orcish-furyxgte",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-orcish-furyxgte-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A fine option if you have an odd-numbered ability score in either Strength or\n\tConstitution (but not both), but this is nothing that you need to have. The\n\tsecond bullet is at most 1d12 extra damage per short rest, and the final\n\tbullet can only trigger once per Short Rest because Relentless Endurance only\n\tworks once per Short Rest."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-prodigyxgte",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "ProdigyXGtE",
      "sourceAnchor": "prodigyxgte",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-prodigyxgte-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Better than the Skilled feat in many ways, but Skill Expert is almost always\n\ta better choice."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-dragonmarks",
      "heading": "Half-Orc Dragonmarks",
      "sourceAnchor": "dragonmarks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dragonmarks-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dragonmarks are detailed in Eberron: Rising from the Last War. Dragonmarks\n\tentirely replace half-orc racial traits, so in many ways dragonmarks should be\n\tthought of as separate races from the Half-Orc. You still have access to the\n\tsame racial feats, but your class options are very different."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-mark-of-finding",
      "parentSectionId": "section-dragonmarks",
      "heading": "Mark of Finding",
      "sourceAnchor": "mark-of-finding",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mark-of-finding-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Constitution and Wisdom is a difficult combination for any class except the\n\tCleric and the Druid. Without an ability boost to either Strength or\n\tDexterity, you’ll lag offensively for a long time. You could make a decent\n\tMonk or Ranger, assuming that you’re fine with being slightly less effective\n\twith your attacks. Mark of Finding’s traits include Darkvision, a bonus to\n\tPerception and Survival, and some interesting innate spellcasting options. If\n\tyour class grants spellcasting, you get access to some interesting\n\tspellcasting. Most of the options are divination spells. Some aren’t available\n\tto clerics, while others aren’t available to druids, and many aren’t available\n\tto rangers, so all three classes get several exciting new options. Whatever\n\tclass you choose, be sure to pick up proficiency in Perception to make\n\tyourself exceptionally observant."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mark-of-finding-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With the custom origin rules in place, it’s hard to decide what to do with\n\tMark of Finding. Access to Hunter’s Mark and Faerie Fire are good options for\n\tthe Warlock. Longstrider is a decent buff on anyone, but not essential. Most\n\tof the other spells are divination options from a combination of the cleric,\n\tdruid, and wizard, so none of the three gain much."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
